# The REX prefix words of 64-bit code, one statement a group, each held to GNU
# as 2.47. Its table has thirty-one for the sixteen bytes 40..4F: `rex` and
# `rex64` with the letters x, y, z behind them (R, X, B), and `rex.` with the
# bits' own letters w, r, x, b. Their bits merge with the ones the instruction
# needs (`rex.x mov %r8d, %ebx` is 46 89 c3), with one another (`rex.w rex.b
# nop` is 49 90) and with a REX2 prefix's (`rex64 addl %r16d, %ebx` is d5 48
# 01 c3); a bit asked for twice is "same type of prefix used twice", and a VEX
# prefix takes none. lccc knew `rex`, `rex.x` and `rex64`, read the dotted
# ones as a mnemonic with a size suffix, and assembled `rex64 mov %rax, %rbx`.
